1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,859
In southern India, the Nilgiri Hills are often called the Blue
Mountains.

2
00:00:04,859 --> 00:00:11,338
Their landscapes are home to many communities, including the
Toda, Kota, Kurumba, Irula, Paniya and Kattunayaka.

3
00:00:11,338 --> 00:00:14,577
Each carries its own living language and traditions.

4
00:00:14,577 --> 00:00:20,651
This is the setting for Hatti Quest, a small attempt to keep a
connection alive.

5
00:00:21,151 --> 00:00:23,590
I'm Siva Babu, and I'm Badaga.

6
00:00:23,590 --> 00:00:28,061
Our community is rooted in the Nilgiri Hills of Tamil Nadu.

7
00:00:28,061 --> 00:00:30,094
Badaga is a Dravidian language.

8
00:00:30,094 --> 00:00:37,004
India's twenty eleven Census recorded one hundred and thirty-
three thousand, five hundred and fifty Badaga mother-tongue
speakers.

9
00:00:37,004 --> 00:00:41,476
That is a dated census count, not a current worldwide estimate.

10
00:00:41,476 --> 00:00:46,354
Behind the number are family conversations, memories, and a
sense of belonging.

11
00:00:46,854 --> 00:00:51,993
Badaga has a strong oral tradition, without a single broadly
standardised native writing system.

12
00:00:51,993 --> 00:00:54,931
That does not mean it cannot be written.

13
00:00:54,931 --> 00:00:59,703
People use Roman, Tamil and Kannada writing, and dedicated
scripts have been proposed.

14
00:00:59,703 --> 00:01:01,906
Spellings and family usage can differ.

15
00:01:01,906 --> 00:01:05,945
A written word alone cannot carry every sound, relationship or
memory.

16
00:01:05,945 --> 00:01:10,717
Preserving the language also means making room for fluent
speakers and familiar voices.

17
00:01:11,217 --> 00:01:17,165
For children growing up outside India, everyday life may happen
mostly in English or another local language.

18
00:01:17,165 --> 00:01:21,014
There may be fewer neighbours, classmates, and relatives who
speak Badaga.

19
00:01:21,014 --> 00:01:25,912
A child can love their roots and still struggle to reply to a
grandparent.

20
00:01:25,912 --> 00:01:29,060
Time zones and busy routines make regular practice harder.

21
00:01:29,060 --> 00:01:33,609
Hatti Quest turns that distance into small, playful
opportunities to connect with family.

22
00:01:34,109 --> 00:01:38,253
The adventure begins on an illustrated trail inspired by the
hills.

23
00:01:38,253 --> 00:01:42,397
Every stop is open, and a lesson takes about three minutes.

24
00:01:42,397 --> 00:01:47,295
Children meet everyday words about family, conversation, nature,
counting, and life at home.

25
00:01:47,295 --> 00:01:52,193
They play gentle recognition games, collect badges, and revisit
words through spaced practice.

26
00:01:52,193 --> 00:01:55,961
There are fifty source-documented words and phrases in this
starter.

27
00:01:55,961 --> 00:02:01,989
The aim is a confident little beginning that can continue after
the screen is put away.

28
00:02:02,489 --> 00:02:06,167
Family corner lets a grown-up record a word in a familiar voice.

29
00:02:06,167 --> 00:02:11,684
A parent or grandparent can say it slowly, then the child can
hear that recording during a lesson.

30
00:02:11,684 --> 00:02:14,749
These short recordings stay in the browser on this device.

31
00:02:14,749 --> 00:02:17,202
They are never uploaded to the AI services.

32
00:02:17,202 --> 00:02:19,041
There is no automatic accent score.

33
00:02:19,041 --> 00:02:25,784
Families can keep their own way of saying a word alongside the
published source form, and download a recording as a keepsake.

34
00:02:26,284 --> 00:02:29,205
Story Studio brings the AI into the experience.

35
00:02:29,205 --> 00:02:35,049
A child chooses a moon mission, an animal adventure, a magical
sketchbook, or a little mystery.

36
00:02:35,049 --> 00:02:37,240
They choose a word topic too.

37
00:02:37,240 --> 00:02:42,718
The system retrieves relevant vocabulary and creates three short
English scenes around those word references.

38
00:02:42,718 --> 00:02:47,466
Python supplies the Badaga forms and the word games from the
documented collection.

39
00:02:47,466 --> 00:02:51,483
A small family activity carries the story back into everyday
life.

40
00:02:51,483 --> 00:02:55,135
An authored adventure remains available if live AI cannot
finish.

41
00:02:55,635 --> 00:02:58,483
The builder's dashboard makes the system visible.

42
00:02:58,483 --> 00:03:01,332
Nebius runs the embedding and story models.

43
00:03:01,332 --> 00:03:08,656
Pinecone searches a dedicated vocabulary namespace, and Python
combines those results with lexical search using reciprocal rank
fusion.

44
00:03:08,656 --> 00:03:13,946
The model returns structured English fiction, then Python
validates the referenced word identifiers.

45
00:03:13,946 --> 00:03:18,829
The run ledger shows the sources, retrieval ranks, latency, and
provider-reported tokens.

46
00:03:18,829 --> 00:03:21,678
A cached adventure reports zero new tokens.

47
00:03:21,678 --> 00:03:26,561
Braintrust traces follow each stage, so a builder can inspect
what happened.

48
00:03:27,061 --> 00:03:30,110
We measured retrieval on twenty-four curated English queries.

49
00:03:30,110 --> 00:03:35,446
Hybrid search found the expected words within its top five
results in every case.

50
00:03:35,446 --> 00:03:38,495
The original lexical search achieved fifty-four percent recall.

51
00:03:38,495 --> 00:03:43,831
Eight generated adventures used valid source word references,
and all six grounding checks passed.

52
00:03:43,831 --> 00:03:48,786
These are small engineering checks, not proof of language
accuracy or learning effectiveness.

53
00:03:48,786 --> 00:03:51,072
Community speaker review is still pending.

54
00:03:51,072 --> 00:03:57,552
Fluent speakers and families are the people who can validate
pronunciation, preferred usage, and the learning experience.

55
00:03:58,052 --> 00:04:03,483
Prometheus scrapes the running Python application, and Grafana
displays the live metrics.

56
00:04:03,483 --> 00:04:10,724
We can see service health, reported tokens, request modes, stage
latency, and the latest evaluation scores.

57
00:04:10,724 --> 00:04:14,345
Braintrust holds the traces and the experiment record.

58
00:04:14,345 --> 00:04:17,966
This makes failures, fallbacks, and cache behaviour inspectable.

59
00:04:17,966 --> 00:04:28,375
The public GitHub repository includes the Python application,
tests, evaluation dataset, and monitoring configuration, so
another builder can reproduce and improve the work.

60
00:04:28,875 --> 00:04:37,362
The next step is community review: more family voices, corrected
word forms, and learning activities shaped by the people who use
the language.

61
00:04:37,362 --> 00:04:42,896
The same approach could support other languages with small
digital collections or strong oral traditions.

62
00:04:42,896 --> 00:04:47,693
Each would need its own source material, permission, speaker
review, and evaluation cases.

63
00:04:47,693 --> 00:04:51,752
It would also need its own choices about writing and
pronunciation.

64
00:04:51,752 --> 00:04:59,500
Hatti Quest is my attempt to make technology useful to
belonging, one little word and one family conversation at a
time.
